<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ARX robotics provider.

  Dispatches by arm variant to the correct bundled xacro under
  robots/arx/<arm>/. Currently supported arms: x5, acone

  Usage:
    xacro robot.xacro robot_model:=arx arm:=x5
    xacro robot.xacro robot_model:=arx arm:=acone
    xacro robot.xacro robot_model:=arx arm:=acone param_source:=sysid

  Both arms accept a `param_source` arg (default | sysid) that selects
  which <model>.params.<param_source>.xacro file supplies the per-joint
  drive gains (<drive stiffness= damping=>) and passive parameters
  (<dynamics damping= friction=>).  `sysid` is the identified set from
  an offline run against ARX Real hardware.  AC one carries the
  left-arm set on both arms - the per-side differences the run produced
  are an artifact of regularization, not of the hardware.  See the
  headers of ../../robots/arx/acone/AC1.params.sysid.xacro (the
  evidence and the caveats) and
  ../../robots/arx/arx_drive_params.sysid.yaml (the armature values,
  command delay and verbatim per-side run output that URDF cannot
  express).

  The same numbers also exist as joint-param overlays, which is where
  new work should read them from:
  ../../robots/arx/acone/overlays/ac1.sysid-2026-09-30.yaml and
  ../../robots/arx/x5/overlays/x5a.sysid-2026-09-30.yaml.  Both carry
  the left-arm fit, with the per-joint notes recording what the right
  arm's own rejected fit produced.  Nothing in the engine consumes an
  overlay yet, so `param_source:=sysid` above is still the only switch
  that has an effect.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:arg name="arm" default="x5" />

  <xacro:property name="arm" value="$(arg arm)" />

  <xacro:if value="${'x5' == arm}">
    <xacro:include filename="$(find genie_sim_robot_model)/robots/arx/x5/X5A.urdf.xacro" />
  </xacro:if>

  <xacro:if value="${'acone' == arm}">
    <xacro:include filename="$(find genie_sim_robot_model)/robots/arx/acone/AC1.urdf.xacro" />
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
